{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779335667,"nonce":"AGhTW2vndKD9z8vbjVA150zxF/A=","title":"Studio: harden task binding semantics across UI, schema, and execution","message":"## Problem\n\nStudio now has a useful task-binding surface: Task Data pseudo-ports can bind into model graph inputs, Model view shows task-input hints, and validation understands task data enough to avoid treating all task connections as ordinary graph wires. That is still not a fully hardened contract. Task bindings are currently spread across workspace scenario state, frontend canvas behavior, schema validation, and execution assumptions.\n\nThe next design step is to make task/model bindings first-class and consistent across Studio, provider schemas, save/load, validation, and worker lowering.\n\n## Context\n\nRecent Model/Task split and task-data binding work established the intended UX:\n\n- task outputs/data are authored in the Task projection, not as normal graph component nodes;\n- task-data edges are editable in Task view but appear only as hints in Model view;\n- Model view should warn before destructive edits that remove nodes/ports with task bindings;\n- task data may expose multiple named bindable variables, such as target position and go/hold cues;\n- those variables can feed model graph inputs through normal model components such as Mux/Concat.\n\nThis issue is about making that semantic boundary durable rather than relying on UI conventions.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the canonical task-binding representation: identity, source task-data id, target node/port, role, value schema, scenario/stage ownership, and persistence behavior.\n- Ensure task bindings are not serialized or interpreted as ordinary graph wires, while still participating in connectivity, validation, selection, deletion, and model-edit warnings.\n- Make task-binding validation produce clear diagnostics tied to visible UI objects and spec paths.\n- Decide how task bindings interact with node deletion, port removal/rename, collapsed nodes, subgraphs, dynamic Mux ports, and Model/Task projection switching.\n- Ensure task bindings round-trip through saved projects, local tabs, workspace snapshots, and imported examples.\n- Provide a frontend/backend contract that worker lowering can consume without ad hoc delayed-reach assumptions.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Task bindings are represented as first-class scenario/workspace objects with stable IDs and schema-backed source/target metadata.\n- UI interactions for create/select/delete task-binding edges behave like graph edges where appropriate, but do not convert them into graph wires.\n- Model view shows task-binding hints and blocks/warns on destructive model edits that invalidate task bindings.\n- Validation treats task data as connected when a task binding supplies a graph input, and reports type/rank/shape compatibility against task data schemas.\n- Save/load and local tab restore preserve task bindings without fallback heuristics.\n- Worker/spec lowering has a documented input contract for task bindings.\n\n## Related Issues\n\n- `bd5aefb` — composable task timelines and expression-valued parameters.\n- `0258fac` — port constraints and type checking.\n- `4178e12` — worker graph/spec lowering and remaining graph-as-model violations.\n- `0e1cd8c` — probe/metric nodes and loss targets.\n- `b3ad156` — interventions/state targeting.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}